  "live_records": {
    "q-23069": {
      "dom_id": "step-q23069",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $3$ times its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $6\\,\\text{in}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $3$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3h \\\\ 2r & =3h \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{2}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3}{2}h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\pi (6)^{2}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =405\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
