      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212195/observations/step-q121586-b14b62e9f7316a27be17.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212195/observations/step-q121586-b14b62e9f7316a27be17.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/tasks/14212195/topics/346/lesson",
      "captured_at": 1792001883.1492262,
      "math_academy_id": "q-121586",
      "topic_id": "346",
      "sequence_position": null,
      "source_step": {
        "dom_id": "step-q121586",
        "placement_id": null,
        "content_id": null,
        "type": null
      },
      "source_example_id": "e-235",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Rate of Change of Radius or Height Given a Rate of Change of Volume",
      "kp_id": "e-235"
    },
    "q-121673": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121673",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212195/assets/cf9fda11d138a5227307070d52fc0a27ce9decc35c91efbb026b89d4507f2ce7.png)\n\nA beverage is pumped into a conical dispenser at a constant rate of  $19.6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The mouth of the tank is $70\\,\\text{cm}$ in diameter and the height of the tank is $40\\,\\text{cm}.$ How fast is the beverage level increasing when the volume of the beverage in the dispenser is $12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. Let's call $H=40\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the tank, and $R=\\frac{70}{2}=35\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the solution level. We call $h$ the solution level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the solution. Both of these quantities, and the solution volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=19.6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the solution must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{35}{40} \\\\ r & =\\frac{7h}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{7h}{8})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{49}{64}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of the beverage in the dispenser is changing when $v=12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.25\\pi  & =\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =48 \\\\ h & =2\\sqrt[3]{6}\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19.6\\pi  & =\\frac{49}{64}\\pi (2\\sqrt[3]{6})^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 19.6\\pi  & =\\frac{49\\sqrt[3]{36}}{16}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & ≈1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the solution level is increasing at a rate of approximately $1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
