    "q-2558": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2558",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212195/assets/a9c0d78ce05859db77dcc1e5198596a1a02c798c607ba9c4ff78e6fe1362b113.png)\n\nThe height of a conical cup is $6\\text{ cm}$ and the opening radius is $2\\text{ cm}.$ Water leaks out of the bottom at the rate of $2\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ If the water level is $3\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the water level decreasing at that moment?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=6\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cup, and $R=2\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{3})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi ({3}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -2 & =\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}.$",
