    "q-51396": {
      "dom_id": "step-q51396",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212195/assets/8676d9a562d254a1194502c5a45368d5345f81aee3a066c28acc87cb1f33812c.png)\n\nThe height of an ice cream cone is $12\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the top of the cone is $2\\,\\text{cm}.$ Ice cream is leaking from a small hole at the bottom at a rate of $2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$ If the ice cream level is $6\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the ice cream level decreasing at that moment?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cone. Let's call $H=12\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cone, and $R=2\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the ice cream level. We call $h$ the height of the ice cream in the cone and $r$ the radius of the ice cream top surface. Both of these quantities, and the ice cream volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of the ice cream:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cone and the ice cream part of the cone must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{2}{12} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{6}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{6})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level in the cone when $h=6\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi ({6}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\,\\text{cm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the ice cream is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
