    "q-2840": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2840",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212195/assets/7732722fc875e8cf13482394990baa0b5f8306a8151fca934491bb12dd181d9e.png)\n\nA  $10\\,\\text{cm}$ tall funnel containing water is being drained at a constant rate of  $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The mouth of the funnel is $12\\,\\text{cm}$ in diameter. How fast is the water level dropping when there is $15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of water remaining in the funnel?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the funnel. Let's call $H=10\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the funnel, and $R=\\frac{12}{2}=6\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the funnel and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{6}{10} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3h}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3h}{5})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of water in the funnel is changing when $v=15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pi  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =125 \\\\ h & =5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi ({5}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -10 & =9\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
