    "q-346630": {
      "dom_id": "step-q346630",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212195/assets/e56e31d999674075f3af880e8eadf14cd978f2dba73dd946dc103aae298f6681.png)\n\nAn inverted right circular conical tank contains sand. The height of the tank is $24\\,\\text{in}$ and the diameter of its top base is $16\\,\\text{in}.$ The sand is draining from the bottom of the tank so that the height of the sand, $h,$ is decreasing at a constant rate of $6\\,\\text{in/h}.$ Find the rate at which the volume of sand is decreasing when the height of the sand is $9\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nHint: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe sand volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. Let's call $H=24\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the conical tank, and $R=\\frac{16}{2}=8\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top base. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the sand level. We call $h$ the height of the sand pile and $r$ the radius of the top surface of the sand. Both of these quantities, and the sand volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=9\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the remaining sand must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{8}{24} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{3}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of sand is changing when $h=9\\,\\text{in}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (9)^{2}(-6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-54\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sand volume is decreasing at a rate of $54\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$",
