    "q-121683": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121683",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212195/assets/f9998b94f05eb6345d375f35f4abc7f88edfc223c4a1af5e7bbc66c4b2a2f0bc.png)\n\nA conical paper cup is $15\\,\\text{m}$ tall with a diameter of $16\\,\\text{m}.$ The cup is being filled with water so that the water level rises at a rate of $1\\,\\text{m/h}.$ At what rate is the water volume increasing when the water level is $10\\,\\text{m}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=15\\,\\text{m}$ the height of the conical cup, and $R=\\frac{16}{2}=8\\,\\text{m}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=10\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{8}{15} \\\\ r & =\\frac{8}{15}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{8}{15}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{64}{675}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{64}{675}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64}{225}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the cup is changing when $h=10\\,\\text{m}.$ \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64}{225}\\pi (10)^{2}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & ≈28.4\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the water volume increases at a rate of $28.4\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
