    "q-2858": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2858",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212195/assets/51ee41eea6dfef1292c68a13a438f43c327639e01452e29998bbe513ac3838ba.png)\n\nA container has the shape of a right circular cone. The height of the container is $20\\,\\text{in}$ and the diameter at the top of the container is $10\\,\\text{in}.$ Water in the container is evaporating so that its depth, $h$, is decreasing at a constant rate of $\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{in/h}.$ At what rate is the volume of water decreasing when the water depth is $12\\,\\text{in}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=20\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=\\frac{10}{2}=5\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=12\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{5}{20} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{4})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the container is changing when $h=12\\,\\text{in}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi (12)^{2}(-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-30\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water is decreasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$",
